 "standard_text": "Solve quadratic equations by completing the square and by the formula; use the discriminant to describe the roots; solve word problems. (summary of the NERDC SSS 2 Mathematics curriculum)",
 "meta_desc": "Free SSS 2 maths worksheets on quadratic equations: completing the square, the quadratic formula, the discriminant and word problems. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) ÷ 2a",
 "key_sub": "b² − 4ac tells how many real roots.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>Write the equation as ax² + bx + c = 0 and read a, b and c with their signs.</li><li>To complete the square, halve the coefficient of x and square it: x² + 6x = (x + 3)² − 9.</li><li>b² − 4ac > 0: two roots; = 0: one repeated root; &lt; 0: no real roots.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>x² − 4x − 1 = 0</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>x = (4 ± √20) ÷ 2</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: x = 4.24 or −0.24</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Losing the sign of b</td><td>−b with b = −4 gives +4.</td></tr><tr><td>Dividing only √ by 2a</td><td>Divide the whole top by 2a.</td></tr><tr><td>Forgetting the ± sign</td><td>There are usually two roots.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
